1
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,920
Hopefully now you understand how the model
sees the world a bit better.

2
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:08,233
With that, we can take a bit more control
and steer

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:10,320
the model towards the things we want it to
do.

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,648
In the end, the only way we can really
control what AI does is by giving

5
00:00:13,648 --> 00:00:16,190
it the right context to do the things we
want it to do,

6
00:00:16,190 --> 00:00:18,640
and that's what this lesson is going to be
all about.

7
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:20,396
Let's start with a simple demo of what

8
00:00:20,396 --> 00:00:22,800
the different harnesses that you might be
using see.

9
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,920
We're going to ask a simple prompt to a
handful of different harnesses and models.

10
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,542
We're asking it to not run any tools and
just list the

11
00:00:28,542 --> 00:00:31,600
blocks of context that it can see right now
from top to bottom.

12
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,200
System prompt tools, skills, etc.

13
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,080
I still remember the days where everyone was
trying to protect their system prompts.

14
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,680
I'm thankful we're over that because it's
good to know

15
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:41,280
what context exists in the model when you're
using it.

16
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:42,960
So we can see exactly that here.

17
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:44,720
Here we have Claude Code's response.

18
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:46,480
It starts with the tool definitions.

19
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:50,529
These are all the things in the harness that
it has access to, like the agent, tool,

20
00:00:50,529 --> 00:00:54,000
bash, edit, read, write, all the things that
the model would want to do.

21
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,539
We then have the system prompt,

22
00:00:55,539 --> 00:00:58,269
which describes the identity that the model
should take

23
00:00:58,269 --> 00:01:00,949
because we want it to behave like a helpful
assistant,

24
00:01:00,949 --> 00:01:04,721
yada, yada, the rules of the harness, the
things it should and shouldn't do,

25
00:01:04,721 --> 00:01:08,741
how we want it to code, how we want it to
speak to us and refer to us and itself,

26
00:01:08,741 --> 00:01:10,429
confirm before risky actions rule,

27
00:01:10,429 --> 00:01:14,400
which is telling it to ask us about doing
things before it does something risky.

28
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:15,253
Context management,

29
00:01:15,253 --> 00:01:18,800
which is a general like how to think about
the things that are in your history.

30
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:22,635
These are all things that the anthropic team
has written in order to

31
00:01:22,635 --> 00:01:26,640
allow Claude to know what capabilities it
has and how it should behave.

32
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,744
The simplest way to think about all of this

33
00:01:28,744 --> 00:01:31,093
is it's almost like the models in Groundhog
Day,

34
00:01:31,093 --> 00:01:34,960
where it wakes up and knows nothing other
than whatever it was taught as a kid.

35
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:38,669
The model has a bunch of knowledge, but it
doesn't really know where it is,

36
00:01:38,669 --> 00:01:40,400
why it's there, or what it's doing.

37
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:41,760
We have to give it all of that.

38
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:45,012
So you can see all of this data here, all of
the system prompt stuff,

39
00:01:45,012 --> 00:01:48,170
almost like a set of sticky notes that we
give to the model when it

40
00:01:48,170 --> 00:01:51,280
wakes up to let it know what's going on and
what its job is to do.

41
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:55,216
There's a bunch more stuff the model's given
access to like my global CLAUDE.md,

42
00:01:55,216 --> 00:01:59,103
the git status snapshot that describes what
the current state of the repo is so

43
00:01:59,103 --> 00:02:02,646
that if you're using cloud code for code it
has that info early and it's

44
00:02:02,646 --> 00:02:04,614
commit and pr attribution lines which is

45
00:02:04,614 --> 00:02:06,779
telling the model how to behave with commits

46
00:02:06,779 --> 00:02:10,617
then we have your message this is what we
actually sent and after our message,

47
00:02:10,617 --> 00:02:12,880
all of these additional pieces come beyond
it.

48
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:14,960
This isn't in the history before our
message.

49
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:16,160
This actually comes after.

50
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,496
This is the environment info, the output
style,

51
00:02:18,496 --> 00:02:21,229
because you can set this in Claude Code, the
tool list,

52
00:02:21,229 --> 00:02:24,757
because remember I said before, mine is on
search mode which means that

53
00:02:24,757 --> 00:02:27,291
the tools aren't always there they're only
there if

54
00:02:27,291 --> 00:02:29,676
the model asks it just has vague
descriptions of

55
00:02:29,676 --> 00:02:32,012
them initially then we have the agent types
all

56
00:02:32,012 --> 00:02:36,186
of the different things that the agent can
do and how it behaves i have a custom one

57
00:02:36,186 --> 00:02:38,223
for Sol because uh it was not easy enough

58
00:02:38,223 --> 00:02:40,560
to let Claude call Sol as a subagent
initially.

59
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:41,200
This fixed it.

60
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:42,080
Probably don't need it anymore.

61
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:43,200
Shadow D3 code.

62
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,840
The MCP servers for things that the model
might want to do.

63
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:50,000
In this case I gave my Claude access to the
ChatGPT computer use server for Codex.

64
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,095
Then we have a skills list auto mode, which
is a thing that the harness has

65
00:02:54,095 --> 00:02:56,880
capability for where it can approve things
for you.

66
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,458
And then the final reminder at the end to be
concise, as well as today's date,

67
00:03:00,458 --> 00:03:03,440
because people sometimes ask and it's good
for the model to know.

68
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:06,629
But again, without all of this, the model
doesn't know anything,

69
00:03:06,629 --> 00:03:09,120
at least not anything about what we want it
to do.

70
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:12,480
This is all the context that we give the
model so that it feels smarter.

71
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:17,600
Because if you just asked a raw instance of
Opus 5.5 to make changes to a code base.

72
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,920
It doesn't even know what code bases are
really.

73
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:21,241
I mean it does to an extent,

74
00:03:21,241 --> 00:03:23,931
like it knows what a GitHub repo is and that
code exists,

75
00:03:23,931 --> 00:03:25,960
but it doesn't know what your code base is,

76
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:28,320
what the state of it is or what's going on
at all.

77
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,204
So by putting all of these things in the

78
00:03:30,204 --> 00:03:32,464
system prompt and in the context the model
gets,

79
00:03:32,464 --> 00:03:34,960
we are steering it to behave in ways that
are better.

80
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:37,209
I also ran this on Codex and you see it's
very

81
00:03:37,209 --> 00:03:40,240
similar in terms of its structure and what
it is putting here.

82
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:43,408
It puts the user message much closer to the
end than Claude Code does,

83
00:03:43,408 --> 00:03:44,720
but it doesn't really matter.

84
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:46,880
They're all behaving effectively the same
way here.

85
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:50,320
But then we have Pi, a beautiful minimal,
elegant option.

86
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:54,480
It has a simple system prompt, a handful of
really simple tools.

87
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:57,120
In this case, read, bash, edit, and write.

88
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:58,560
It then has available skills.

89
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,021
These are the ones that are provided by my
setup,

90
00:04:01,021 --> 00:04:02,880
which has a very small set of things.

91
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:04,320
It has my repo explorer skill.

92
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:07,697
I use railway skill for dealing with my
railway deployments, PyDS4 config,

93
00:04:07,697 --> 00:04:10,800
which came from something I installed a
while ago, you get the idea.

94
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:12,320
Then we have the project instructions.

95
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:14,880
These are the things that are included for a
specific project.

96
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:18,720
So if you have a CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md in
the repo, this gets added there.

97
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:20,400
We'll talk about those in just a bit.

98
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:21,962
We have the project context,

99
00:04:21,962 --> 00:04:25,589
which is environment info it automatically
pulls in when you run,

100
00:04:25,589 --> 00:04:29,160
like the current directory, other little
bits of info like that,

101
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:31,280
and then the user messages at the end.

102
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:32,799
I want to talk a bit more about

103
00:04:32,799 --> 00:04:35,395
this groundhog day effect because I think
it's really

104
00:04:35,395 --> 00:04:37,404
important to understand as we try to make

105
00:04:37,404 --> 00:04:40,000
our models more capable at delivering real
good code.

106
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Something weird about how I work is that I
really don't like long-lived threads.

107
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,343
I try to keep all my threads as effectively
ephemeral as possible with

108
00:04:47,343 --> 00:04:50,160
the goal of closing it as soon as possible
after I open it.

109
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:52,560
But as we've been discussing, the history is
important.

110
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:54,094
If something isn't in the history,

111
00:04:54,094 --> 00:04:56,486
the model doesn't know about it and then it
has to do

112
00:04:56,486 --> 00:04:59,600
things like call tools or hallucinate in
order to get the next steps.

113
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,141
And we don't want it to hallucinate and we
don't want it to have to

114
00:05:02,141 --> 00:05:03,582
do a bunch of tool calls before making

115
00:05:03,582 --> 00:05:05,440
simple changes that it should already know
about.

116
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:07,657
Once you start working on lots of things in
parallel,

117
00:05:07,657 --> 00:05:10,000
you're going to start experiencing some
weird behaviors.

118
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,212
Like you have one thread that got something
wrong, you correct it,

119
00:05:13,212 --> 00:05:16,960
then you go to one of the other threads and
it also got the same thing wrong.

120
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:18,640
That can be annoying.

121
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,011
The reason for this is because the

122
00:05:20,011 --> 00:05:22,229
model isn't sharing the information across
the threads,

123
00:05:22,229 --> 00:05:23,520
it has to get it from somewhere.

124
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:27,280
And the best place the model can get this
info is from the code itself.

125
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:29,440
Let's see what this looks like with another
demo.

126
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:32,160
Here we're going to start a new session
called session one.

127
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,520
We're telling it that we're making some
changes to our cart system.

128
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:39,680
We're adding discount codes and a receipt to
this cart in two steps.

129
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:42,480
The first step is that we're adding this
particular code.

130
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:45,760
We are adding this coupon code for saving
10%.

131
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:49,280
And then step two is that we should add this
new receipt function.

132
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:51,920
Returns one string, yada, yada, yada.

133
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:55,600
We're going to tell it to only do step one
and do tests for it.

134
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:57,890
If we were to do that and then start another
thread,

135
00:05:57,890 --> 00:05:59,520
it wouldn't know where things are at.

136
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:03,848
It could like read the code, maybe it could
even use its tools to find the history

137
00:06:03,848 --> 00:06:06,540
of your chats on the computer to build this
all up,

138
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:09,760
but ideally it could find what it needs from
the code itself.

139
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:12,080
So in this case, we're going to fire this
off.

140
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:16,240
It's going to make the change for this one
part of the two part request.

141
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:18,451
So it started by doing a bunch of tool
calls,

142
00:06:18,451 --> 00:06:21,840
in this case reading the files that like the
AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md.

143
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:24,960
It doesn't have to do that because they're
included in the context by default,

144
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:27,200
but if it doesn't know, it can go double
check this way.

145
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:30,720
We then output some text and write it to
Cart.js and then

146
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:34,240
we write a bit more text to HANDOFF.md to
wrap things up.

147
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,960
Step one is now done and all eight tests
pass.

148
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:38,720
I then stopped as you asked.

149
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:41,180
It made the changes, it updated the tests,

150
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:45,281
and then it wrote to the HANDOFF.md file
explaining what things are at

151
00:06:45,281 --> 00:06:49,440
and restating step two so that some other
thread can pick this work up.

152
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:52,560
Obviously if we were working traditionally
these two tasks are similar enough,

153
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:55,280
they should probably just be done in the
same thread or in one shot.

154
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,686
But if you have complex work that you'll

155
00:06:57,686 --> 00:07:00,873
be working on across many threads over large
windows,

156
00:07:00,873 --> 00:07:04,000
encoding what matters in the code base is
important.

157
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,360
This is the contents of that HANDOFF.md file
if you were curious by the way.

158
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:09,840
We can see it has a simple markdown file
with a title.

159
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:12,000
It says the work split into two steps.

160
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,081
It says the basis of the project says what
it already did,

161
00:07:15,081 --> 00:07:18,480
and then it says what step two is so the
model can do that next.

162
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:22,472
We press enter, session two starts and we
give it a very simple request,

163
00:07:22,472 --> 00:07:24,800
continue the work described in handoff.md.

164
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:28,184
And now, despite not having any of the
history from before,

165
00:07:28,184 --> 00:07:31,224
it's able to operate almost as though it
does because

166
00:07:31,224 --> 00:07:34,320
the context that it needed was it read from
this file.

167
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:37,040
We're not even including the file in the
history ourselves.

168
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:40,560
We are just telling the model the file
exists and that it has what it needs.

169
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:43,465
So it runs this cat command to read the
content,

170
00:07:43,465 --> 00:07:46,673
which outputs the content into its history
and now it

171
00:07:46,673 --> 00:07:49,760
knows it can do things and what is requested
of it.

172
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:51,386
It then generates the code,

173
00:07:51,386 --> 00:07:54,880
it tells us all about what it changed and
now we are done.

174
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:57,069
And you can see the effect here which is

175
00:07:57,069 --> 00:07:59,970
that session one used 5,000 tokens and
session 2 only

176
00:07:59,970 --> 00:08:02,433
had about 500 because it didn't have to go
do

177
00:08:02,433 --> 00:08:05,280
all of these things and get all of this
information.

178
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:06,960
It could just output a simple thing.

179
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:10,033
Now that we understand the basics of how the
model gets the context,

180
00:08:10,033 --> 00:08:12,880
I'm gonna say some things that might sound a
bit contradictory.

181
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:14,689
We shouldn't be giving all of these

182
00:08:14,689 --> 00:08:17,429
detailed instructions in our shared global
AGENTS.md,

183
00:08:17,429 --> 00:08:19,600
CLAUDE.md, or even the project level ones.

184
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:23,505
Anything that the model can get easily with
one command probably doesn't belong in

185
00:08:23,505 --> 00:08:25,791
your CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md files because
models

186
00:08:25,791 --> 00:08:27,600
can run commands and get that context.

187
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:29,853
The reason that you put things in these
shared

188
00:08:29,853 --> 00:08:32,547
context files is to share knowledge that is
not trivial

189
00:08:32,547 --> 00:08:35,045
to get and more importantly to steer model
behavior

190
00:08:35,045 --> 00:08:37,200
in the directions that you want it to go in.

191
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:41,280
For reference, here is the actual ClaudeMD
from T3 code.

192
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:43,783
We give a basic description of what T3 code
is so that

193
00:08:43,783 --> 00:08:46,240
it knows what it's working on as soon as it
wakes up.

194
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:49,280
I then chose to tell the model what makes T3
code special.

195
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:52,608
The reason I did this is I noticed the model
would make changes that

196
00:08:52,608 --> 00:08:56,720
didn't always embrace the core promises that
I think are so important to my project.

197
00:08:56,720 --> 00:09:00,166
In this case, that it's open at the core,
that it's always performing very well,

198
00:09:00,166 --> 00:09:01,760
so don't do things that slow it down.

199
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:02,575
It's remote ready,

200
00:09:02,575 --> 00:09:06,105
so any and all changes and new features we
add should work remotely as well as

201
00:09:06,105 --> 00:09:09,816
on your local device and that it's multi-
surface so that it will work well on web,

202
00:09:09,816 --> 00:09:10,720
desktop, and mobile.

203
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:13,559
I have a note for myself, which might sound
crazy,

204
00:09:13,559 --> 00:09:16,909
but this helps the model understand how I am
thinking about

205
00:09:16,909 --> 00:09:21,680
things so that when I or others contribute
to this code base, this context is given.

206
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:24,803
You can get the equivalent experience from
the model by copy-pasting

207
00:09:24,803 --> 00:09:27,466
this text at the start before you send your
first message,

208
00:09:27,466 --> 00:09:29,670
but I'd much rather the model just know what
I'm

209
00:09:29,670 --> 00:09:32,655
going for and also channel specific
behaviors like measure twice,

210
00:09:32,655 --> 00:09:34,400
cut once, and you ain't gonna need it.

211
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:38,428
These things are all small and you'll notice
a lot of this is almost vibes based,

212
00:09:38,428 --> 00:09:41,263
but I'm trying to vibe the model into the
direction where

213
00:09:41,263 --> 00:09:43,600
I live so that it behaves the ways that I
want.

214
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:46,003
One of the best ways to improve here is to
pay attention

215
00:09:46,003 --> 00:09:48,320
to when the model does things you don't want
it to do.

216
00:09:48,320 --> 00:09:51,771
Like it takes too long to complete a task or
it uses the wrong tool or edits a

217
00:09:51,771 --> 00:09:53,851
file it shouldn't have edited or pushes code
it

218
00:09:53,851 --> 00:09:56,240
shouldn't have or it doesn't push code it
should have.

219
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:59,280
Ask the model why it did the thing after it
does it.

220
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:02,800
And then from there you can find
opportunities to improve these files.

221
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:04,580
You might also have noticed there's
something

222
00:10:04,580 --> 00:10:06,320
missing from everything we looked at so far.

223
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:09,360
There isn't a single code path anywhere here
so far.

224
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:11,399
The reason for that is because code paths
are

225
00:10:11,399 --> 00:10:13,801
not that important if the model can find
them itself,

226
00:10:13,801 --> 00:10:15,840
and these models are very, very good at
that.

227
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:18,640
What they're less good at is communicating
in the ways that we communicate.

228
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:22,263
So if you and your team have a specific
grammar or set of terms you use,

229
00:10:22,263 --> 00:10:24,880
defining that in one of these files is very
helpful.

230
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:26,918
So you don't get confused when the model
says

231
00:10:26,918 --> 00:10:29,680
something in three different ways in three
different threads.

232
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:31,458
You now have a shared grammar where you

233
00:10:31,458 --> 00:10:33,600
and the model can understand each other
better.

234
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:35,328
These types of glossaries have been way more

235
00:10:35,328 --> 00:10:36,978
helpful than I ever would have expected in

236
00:10:36,978 --> 00:10:40,160
my code bases and I've started introducing
them into a lot of the work that I do.

237
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:42,324
There are other ways for the model to get
context and

238
00:10:42,324 --> 00:10:44,978
if you put all of its capabilities in the
CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md,

239
00:10:44,978 --> 00:10:48,080
you're just going to bloat the context up
with things that might not matter.

240
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:50,790
For example, I want the model to be able to
spin up a

241
00:10:50,790 --> 00:10:54,114
dev server on Tailscale if I'm using a
remote box over Tailscale,

242
00:10:54,114 --> 00:10:57,489
but I don't want it to just blindly run
Tailscale commands all the

243
00:10:57,489 --> 00:11:01,120
time and to have all this context when it
doesn't work or isn't needed.

244
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:02,872
Like from running on my local machine,

245
00:11:02,872 --> 00:11:05,040
I don't want it to fire off Tailscale
commands.

246
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:06,880
And this is where skills come in.

247
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,291
Again, I'm going to use a real example from
the

248
00:11:09,291 --> 00:11:11,600
T3 code code base with our test T3 app
skill.

249
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:14,570
I have a repo called Fleet where I
personally manage all of my skills,

250
00:11:14,570 --> 00:11:17,200
context that's shared across projects and
all of those things.

251
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:18,708
And I'm going to look at one of my

252
00:11:18,708 --> 00:11:21,680
personal favorite skills I've been using a
lot recently, Full Stop.

253
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:24,537
This is a skill I built for when I want the
agent

254
00:11:24,537 --> 00:11:27,744
to go all the way end to end to figure out
the problem,

255
00:11:27,744 --> 00:11:32,000
verify it itself, iterate loop accordingly,
and then make the thing work.

256
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,120
It should put up a PR,

257
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:37,142
continue monitoring the PR until it is ready
to merge as far as it's concerned,

258
00:11:37,142 --> 00:11:39,280
and then stop and tell me that it's ready.

259
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:41,493
The reason I call it full stop is that you
do

260
00:11:41,493 --> 00:11:44,640
everything it possibly can until merge and
that's when it stops.

261
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:46,800
Skills have a few core pieces.

262
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:49,727
They have a name, a description, optional
metadata,

263
00:11:49,727 --> 00:11:52,080
and then the actual content of the skill.

264
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:56,769
I'm gonna give a hot take quick and say that
description is a bad name for the

265
00:11:56,769 --> 00:11:59,835
description because it's implying that the
point of

266
00:11:59,835 --> 00:12:02,240
this section is to tell us what it does.

267
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:06,072
It isn't that at all the point of the
description is to tell the

268
00:12:06,072 --> 00:12:10,203
model when it should use the thing I think
the better phrase for this

269
00:12:10,203 --> 00:12:14,514
would be trigger not description and that's
how I use it you'll see here

270
00:12:14,514 --> 00:12:18,825
I say use when *** asks for full stop or
explicitly invokes this skill,

271
00:12:18,825 --> 00:12:20,800
not when he asks to stop working.

272
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:23,813
I almost want to go change this and I
probably will when I'm done

273
00:12:23,813 --> 00:12:27,614
recording to literally just say use when
*** asks for full stop and nothing else,

274
00:12:27,614 --> 00:12:29,840
because that is how descriptions should be
used.

275
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:32,320
They are what lets the model know the point
of the skill.

276
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:36,000
The reason this all matters is the model
chooses when the skill comes in.

277
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,680
When you write a skill, it's not just always
there bloating your context.

278
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:41,760
The model sees a very small amount of data.

279
00:12:41,920 --> 00:12:44,960
It just sees the name and the description
and nothing else.

280
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:48,422
So if you want the model to decide when to
pull the skill in itself,

281
00:12:48,422 --> 00:12:51,680
you would give it a description that
categorizes when it should.

282
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:54,352
If you want the model to only do a thing
when you specify,

283
00:12:54,352 --> 00:12:56,240
then you can say that in the description.

284
00:12:56,240 --> 00:12:59,416
In this case, I only want the skill to run
when I say full stop and I

285
00:12:59,416 --> 00:13:02,960
don't have to like carefully do the dollar
sign full dash stop to trigger it.

286
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:05,280
I just want the model to pull it in when it
should.

287
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:08,960
And then we have the important part of the
skill, the actual text content.

288
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:11,040
In this case, I describe what this is.

289
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:14,397
***'s not watching, own the work until the
PR is ready for him to merge,

290
00:13:14,397 --> 00:13:16,880
keep fixing, verifying, and reviewing, never
merge it.

291
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:18,907
And then I describe all of the things I

292
00:13:18,907 --> 00:13:21,454
wanted to do in this particular style of
working,

293
00:13:21,454 --> 00:13:24,105
but this is all the context that the model
needs to

294
00:13:24,105 --> 00:13:27,120
behave the way I want when I call out this
specific thing.

295
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:28,960
You can do this for lots of different
things.

296
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:32,880
For example, my leaving PR comments skill is
one of my other favorites.

297
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:38,000
This one is how I describe to the model the
format to use when it leaves comments.

298
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,836
I could manually invoke the skill by saying
use the

299
00:13:40,836 --> 00:13:44,228
leaving PR comment skill or running the
command to invoke it,

300
00:13:44,228 --> 00:13:46,897
but here I have a more vague description
where I

301
00:13:46,897 --> 00:13:50,122
say that it should use this whenever an
agent is drafting,

302
00:13:50,122 --> 00:13:53,848
posting, replying to, or resolving PRs or
review comments for ***,

303
00:13:53,848 --> 00:13:56,240
including comments made by other PR skills.

304
00:13:56,240 --> 00:13:59,604
And here I describe the exact format I want
the model to leave comments in,

305
00:13:59,604 --> 00:14:01,219
if you go look at something like the

306
00:14:01,219 --> 00:14:03,506
T3 code GitHub or any other place I'm
contributing,

307
00:14:03,506 --> 00:14:05,614
you'll see all the comments left in this
format

308
00:14:05,614 --> 00:14:08,305
because the models are smart enough to see
the subscription,

309
00:14:08,305 --> 00:14:10,817
say I should probably use that if I'm
leaving a comment,

310
00:14:10,817 --> 00:14:12,880
pull it into context and then act
accordingly.

311
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:14,400
This is what makes skills so useful.

312
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:15,680
They're super dynamic.

313
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:17,760
The model can pull them in on different
conditions.

314
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:19,174
You can manually pull them in,

315
00:14:19,174 --> 00:14:22,426
and all of this allows the model to change
how it behaves because the

316
00:14:22,426 --> 00:14:24,547
context is different depending on what
skills

317
00:14:24,547 --> 00:14:26,480
you're using and when they're being used.

318
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:29,308
If there are certain behaviors or issues
that you notice

319
00:14:29,308 --> 00:14:32,288
you and your coworkers are running into on a
given project,

320
00:14:32,288 --> 00:14:35,319
you can use a combination of the CLAUDE.md,
AGENTS.md files,

321
00:14:35,319 --> 00:14:38,400
as well as custom skills to steer the model
in the right way.

322
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:38,895
Separately,

323
00:14:38,895 --> 00:14:42,495
if you have specific behaviors or
characteristics you want the model to
exhibit,

324
00:14:42,495 --> 00:14:45,375
for example, you don't want it to bother you
until the PR is up,

325
00:14:45,375 --> 00:14:47,445
or you want to make sure it uses browser use
a

326
00:14:47,445 --> 00:14:49,785
bunch to test your changes before it puts up
the PR,

327
00:14:49,785 --> 00:14:52,215
you can make these all personal custom
skills that the

328
00:14:52,215 --> 00:14:54,960
model will have access to when it's running
on your machines,

329
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:56,940
but these won't affect how your teammates do

330
00:14:56,940 --> 00:14:59,280
things when they pull the code base down
themselves.

331
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:01,202
One last thing I'll say here and I feel bad

332
00:15:01,202 --> 00:15:03,840
because I've kind of broken the rule a bit
myself recently.

333
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:06,960
I don't love writing skills with agents.

334
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,253
I'll often have them make the first version
of the

335
00:15:09,253 --> 00:15:11,592
skill and then send it across my fleet of
machines,

336
00:15:11,592 --> 00:15:14,069
but I'll almost always go back in and clean
things up,

337
00:15:14,069 --> 00:15:17,600
just deleting a bunch of useless text and
things that don't matter that much.

338
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:22,480
LLMs love to be unnecessarily verbose and
skills should be relatively simple.

339
00:15:22,480 --> 00:15:24,080
The less text the better.

340
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:25,760
The simpler the instructions the better.

341
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:29,604
And if you give it a simple flow like do
one, two, three, four, five,

342
00:15:29,604 --> 00:15:32,000
the model almost always behave accordingly.

343
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,128
Skills shouldn't be treated as this esoteric

344
00:15:34,128 --> 00:15:36,160
black box that you only touch with agents.

345
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:40,053
You should spend a decent bit of time
customizing them, configuring them,

346
00:15:40,053 --> 00:15:43,680
seeing how they behave and how small changes
change their behaviors.

347
00:15:43,680 --> 00:15:45,564
This is how you control the way the model

348
00:15:45,564 --> 00:15:48,000
works with you and you should take advantage
of that.

349
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,678
One last thing I want to touch on with
context is the

350
00:15:50,678 --> 00:15:53,760
types of things the model can see because
it's not just text.

351
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:55,471
Different models have different
capabilities,

352
00:15:55,471 --> 00:15:57,600
but there's a handful that pretty much all
of them have.

353
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:00,716
For example, pretty much every model you're
going to be using with

354
00:16:00,716 --> 00:16:03,360
code today has the ability to see and
understand images.

355
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:06,240
I'll show this by taking a screenshot of T3
code itself.

356
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:09,029
I use a tool called Shotter in order to
annotate my screenshots and

357
00:16:09,029 --> 00:16:12,400
I'm gonna show you something that honestly
is still just like mind-blowing to me.

358
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:16,074
I'm gonna point an arrow at the icon here
where I have the brain

359
00:16:16,074 --> 00:16:19,920
in the word high because I don't love that
we show this brain icon.

360
00:16:19,920 --> 00:16:25,036
We all know what high means now, so I'm
going to say something in here like ugly,

361
00:16:25,036 --> 00:16:25,920
get rid of it.

362
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:30,915
Now I can take this image I made, put it on
my clipboard, paste it to the model,

363
00:16:30,915 --> 00:16:33,600
and without saying anything else, hit send.

364
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:35,681
Now the model is thinking about this image,

365
00:16:35,681 --> 00:16:38,240
trying to pinpoint exactly where the arrow
is pointing.

366
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:40,291
It looks like it's near the bottom bar area,

367
00:16:40,291 --> 00:16:43,600
possibly indicating the high brain icon just
below the composer's edge.

368
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:46,515
It then greps for brain in the code base,
reads the picker,

369
00:16:46,515 --> 00:16:50,320
which is the code where this lives, and
decides what it wants to do about it.

370
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:51,280
It removed the icon.

371
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:52,944
Dependencies are not installed within the
work tree,

372
00:16:52,944 --> 00:16:54,640
so I'm installing them now to run lint and
typecheck.

373
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:57,520
It's now running commands in order to test
these things that it changed.

374
00:16:57,520 --> 00:17:02,000
But this image that I sent it gave it the
context it needed to know what to do.

375
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,160
I sent no text along with it.

376
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:08,213
T3 code will automatically insert this user-
attached one or more files text,

377
00:17:08,213 --> 00:17:12,640
just because some models and harnesses need
some text, but it isn't even necessary.

378
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:14,698
This image is just another chunk of data
that's

379
00:17:14,698 --> 00:17:16,799
in the history as far as the model is
concerned,

380
00:17:16,799 --> 00:17:19,295
which means that all the other techniques
we've given the

381
00:17:19,295 --> 00:17:21,440
model to add things to its history work here
too.

382
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:24,876
For example, if the model makes a UI change
and it wants to confirm it,

383
00:17:24,876 --> 00:17:26,812
it can use a tool to spin up the change,

384
00:17:26,812 --> 00:17:29,231
it can use another tool to open it in the
browser,

385
00:17:29,231 --> 00:17:33,199
then it can use one last tool call to get a
screenshot of the result that it would

386
00:17:33,199 --> 00:17:35,135
then get in its history as a new message

387
00:17:35,135 --> 00:17:37,360
that it can look at and make decisions
around.

388
00:17:37,360 --> 00:17:38,212
To wrap things up,

389
00:17:38,212 --> 00:17:41,858
it's important to understand that the model
only has so much knowledge in it,

390
00:17:41,858 --> 00:17:45,600
and its behavior doesn't come from its
training so much as what context it has.

391
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:48,400
Obviously the training matters, obviously
the model matters,

392
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:51,760
but what it knows and what it has access to
matters as much if not more.

393
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:53,527
The context the agent has is one of the

394
00:17:53,527 --> 00:17:55,974
most important things we have to control its
behavior,

395
00:17:55,974 --> 00:17:59,600
which is why it's important to understand it
and start working with it directly.

396
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:03,108
You can use the tools we showed here, things
like skills, CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md,

397
00:18:03,108 --> 00:18:05,290
and all these other ways to give the model
context,

398
00:18:05,290 --> 00:18:07,515
even just changing what files are where in
your code

399
00:18:07,515 --> 00:18:09,440
base to make it easier for the model to
find.

400
00:18:09,440 --> 00:18:11,520
All of this is what steers the way the model
behaves.

401
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:15,459
And if you ever notice the model doing
things you don't like or that are confusing,

402
00:18:15,459 --> 00:18:17,642
you can ask why it did it and what
information

403
00:18:17,642 --> 00:18:19,920
it had in its context that made it act that
way.

404
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:23,094
You can always just ask the model what info
it sees and what

405
00:18:23,094 --> 00:18:26,480
it has and get good answers to figure out
how it sees the world.

406
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:28,781
Because something might be obvious to us
when we look

407
00:18:28,781 --> 00:18:30,474
at the app or we look at the code base,

408
00:18:30,474 --> 00:18:32,775
but the model doesn't know it until it runs
the right

409
00:18:32,775 --> 00:18:35,120
command to get the data that it needs to do
the thing.

410
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:37,006
As models keep getting better,

411
00:18:37,006 --> 00:18:40,841
they are improving their ability to build
context themselves,

412
00:18:40,841 --> 00:18:44,676
but it's still important to send them in the
right direction,

413
00:18:44,676 --> 00:18:49,392
and all of these lessons will help you
understand why the models behave the

414
00:18:49,392 --> 00:18:53,920
way they do and change the behavior to make
them more effective for you.
